{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} LOGGING_IN}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} default}
\b Logging in\b0\par
\sb75

When launching the program for the first time or after you logged out, the process of creating the authorization key 
takes place. It might take a while depending on how fast your CPU is. Afterwards, the "Log In" dialog will be shown. 
You have two options:\par

1.Enter your phone number and press "Get code". A 5-digit code will be sent to your Telegram account. 
Input it in the code field and press "Log in".\par
2. Scan the QR code using the QR code scanner of a Telegram client where you're logged in to your account.\par

If your account has the two-factor authentication turned out, you'll be asked for your password prior to getting 
logged in. When you input your password and press "Log in", the SRP algorithm of checking your password will begin. 
Unfortunately, there is a 30-second timer set by Telegram, and because the algorithm is quite heavy, it might not 
get finished in time if your CPU is too slow. The timer also starts when you check your hint for the first time 
each session, so if you try to log in before it finishes, you have a higher chance of timing out as well.\par

Note: after pressing "Get code" or scanning the QR code, the process of creating the authorization key will happen 
again if your account is located on the American or Asian Telegram datacenter. This is because by default Telegacy 
connects and creates the authorization key on the European datacenter.\par

Note 2: You can't register an account with Telegacy. This feature is reserved only for the official Telegram client.\par

\page

\sb115
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} PROGRAM_FILES}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} default}
\b Program files\b0\par
\sb75

Together with the executable you have:\par
1. "help.hlp", "help.cnt" files. The WinHelp version of this user guide.\par
2. "help.chm" file. The HTML Help version of this user guide. Is used if the 
WinHelp version is missing or can't be opened.\par
3. "emoji_categories.dat" file. This file contains the different emoji categories and the names of the emojis 
that are a part of each category. If you want, you can edit this file and change the categories' contents. 
You can find the structure of this file in the source code.\par
4. "emojis" folder. This file contains emojis in the .ico format. Telegacy ships with the Forumoji emojis. 
You can add missing emojis or replace the existing ones.\par
5. "langs" folder. Contains INI localization files in ANSI encoding for different languages. 
Feel free to make changes or add new languages.\par
6. "emoji.ini" file. Has aliases for all the emojis, so you can, for example, type :heart: and the heart emoji 
will be pasted. Once again, you can do any changes you want.\par\par

Also, Telegacy creates new user files in %appdata%/Telegacy/ (in case your system doesn't have %appdata%, 
"%windir%/Application Data/Telegacy/" will be used). Those include:\par
1. "DCs.dat" file. Contains the IP addresses and ports of all the Telegam datacenters, It also tells which datacenter 
the current logged in account is on.\par
2. "session.dat" file. Has everything needed for an access to your account. Updates when you exit the program, 
except for the sequence number, which is written to this file every 10th time it changes, this measure is needed for the 
prevention of bad consequences in case of unexpected crashes.\par
3. "database.dat" file. Includes data about your chats and folders, as well as the default reactions and themes. 
Updates when you exit the program.\par
4. "fav_emojis.dat" file. Is a list of the emojis you used. Can have a maximum of 40 emojis.\par
5. "options.ini" file. Your options are here.\par
6. "custom_emojis" folder. The custom emojis you encounter get downloaded to this folder.\par\par

When you log out, the "DCs.dat", "session.dat" and "database.dat" files get deleted.\par

\page

\sb115
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} DOWNLOADING_FILES}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} default}
\b Downloading files\b0\par
\sb75

The files in chats are shown as links. Click on one to start downloading the file you need. The files are downloaded 
in chunks of 1 MB each, and you can see the progress on the status bar. In case a file is located on a different 
datacenter from yours and you haven't downloaded anything from it before, you'll have to wait through the process of 
creating an authorization key for it. You can also download the full sizes of images by clicking on their right halves.\par

\page

\sb115
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} UPLOADING_FILES}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} default}
\b Uploading files\b0\par
\sb75

Click on the attachment button on the toolbar to select the files you want to upload. After selecting the files and 
pressing "OK", they'll be added to the list, with the attachment button getting a red indicator. It's also possible 
to drag and drop files into the application. You can add more or remove some by clicking on them in the list. 
"Clear this list" option removes all of them. The files will start to be uploaded after sending the message, 
with the progress shown in the status bar, and the list will be cleared.\par

Note: if the version of your comctl32.dll is 4.70 or lower, you won't get the dropdown arrow next to the attachment button. 
To open the list of files selected for uploading, choose the "Show the uploading list" option in the Tools menu instead.\par

By default, multimedia gets sent as, well, multimedia. However, you can send it as simple files if you check 
"Send media as files" in the Tools menu. This also allows you to send lots of different files at once, when not 
possible otherwise.\par

\page

\sb115
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} OPERATIONS_WITH_MESSAGES}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} default}
\b Operations with messages\b0\par
\sb75

There are several things you can do with messages by right clicking on them:\par

1. Editing. Lets you edit your messages. Choosing this option adds the editing button to the toolbar as an indicator. 
Click on it to cancel editing, or send the message to finish editing.\par
2. Replying. Sending a message in the replying state will result in a message which is a reply to the message you 
picked. Just like when editing, you'll have the replying button on the toolbar.\par
3. Forwarding. Allows you to forward messages. The forwarding state is accompained by the forwarding button 
and stays when going into a different chat, which lets you forward messages to different chats.\par
4. Deleting. Deletes messages. You can choose whether to delete the specified message for yourself only or for 
everyone. Be careful, there's no warning!\par
5. Reacting. Right click specifically on the message footer for the reaction selector to appear.\par

\page

\sb115
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} PROFILES}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} default}
\b Profiles\b0\par
\sb75

You can see your own profile by choosing the "View profile" option in the Profile menu, or the profile of a chat 
by choosing the same option in the Chat menu. The following information is displayed there: profile picture, name, 
handle, birthday and bio. You can change your data or data of a group or channel you own by editing the fields. To 
change the profile picture, click on it and choose an image you want. If you want to not show the year of your 
birthday, choose a date of the current year.\par

\page

\sb115
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} OPTIONS}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} default}
\b Options\b0\par
\sb75

Telegacy has several options. The options you choose get applied no matter whether you press the "OK", "Cancel" 
or the close button.\par\par

\b General\b0\par

1. Language. The client's language.\par
2. Messages fetch count. How many messages get fetched when opening the chat and browsing chat history.\par
3. Current download directory. The files you download go there.\par
4. Minimize to tray when closing. This makes the program continue to run in the background after closing it. 
To actually close it, go to Profile -> Exit on the main menu; the Profile menu is also shown when right clicking 
on the tray icon.\par
5. Use Windows notifications instead of the custom ones. This way, the notifications are shown in the default way 
for Windows. When unchecked, the Telegacy's custom notifications will be used instead, which is the only possible 
way if the version of your shell32.dll file is lower than 5.0. If your version is 5.x, the custom notifications 
bring the additional functionality of being able to click on them to go to the respective chat. With Windows 
notifications it's only possible if your version is 6.0 or above.\par
6. Check for updates on launch. The program will check for updates on startup by connecting to the host server at 
http://webdav.nixxo.net/telegacy/. Special thanks to NixxO for hosting.\par\par

\b Content\b0\par

1. How to load images in chats?\par

\li345
\f1\bullet\f0  Don't load images at all. Shows images as rectangle symbols.\par
\f1\bullet\f0  Load just the minimal image thumbnails. Telegram sends image messages together with a very small preview, 
which this option shows.\par
\f1\bullet\f0  Autodownload and load the bigger sized image thumbnails. The said bigger size is up to 300x300 pixels. 
These thumbnails don't get downloaded to disk, they stay in memory until the chat is closed. 
This option also shows stickers.\par
\li115
2. Enable emojis. When unchecked, emojis are shown as rectangle symbols, and the emoji button on the toolbar 
gets grayed out.\par
3. Apply appropriate formatting to spoilers in chats. Makes the text with the "spoiler" formatting have a black 
background. Since the text is black, it makes it not visible, in order to see it you need to select it. Since 
it's a bit of an ugly workaround, you can disable it altogether.\par\par

\b Theme\b0\par

1. Main color. The background color of the main window.\par
2. Chat color. The background color of the chat, the message input, folder and chat comboboxes, 
fields in the Profile dialog window.\par
3. Dialog color. The background color of dialog windows, as well as of the emoji and reaction choosers.\par
4. Text color. The color of all the text.\par
5. Individual chat themes. If a Telegram chat theme is set, this will change the main color to 
the theme's color when opening said chat.\par
6. Chat font. Also the font of the message input control and the profiles' "About me" field.\par
7. Chat list font. Also the font of the folder list and most of the fields of the Profile window.\par
8. UI font.\par\par

\b Sounds\b0\par

Here you can select the sounds you want for the new message notifications, as well as for the incoming and 
outgoing messages in the current chat. The sounds have to be in the .wav format. By default, the Windows' 
notify.wav sound is used for new message notifications; incoming/outgoing messages don't have a sound.\par\par

\b Voice\b0\par

Here you can choose the sample rate, the amount of bits per sample and channels used 
when recording your voice messages.\par\par

\b Proxy\b0\par

Here you can set up your connection to a SOCKS5 proxy. If the IP or port aren't set, the client won't be using a proxy. 
Username and password are needed if the proxy of your choice requires authentication. They don't matter if the proxy 
doesn't need any credentials.\par

\page

